# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
universal_law	one
nobody_believes	one
promise_possible	one
# promise_possible: A1 could be read as needing only the utterance of promise-words, while P2 denies that a promise can be received as one. But the maxim's own action, "promise to repay," needs a promise that lenders accept, since otherwise nothing is lent. Both statements are therefore about the same thing: whether a promise can really be made. I have not counted this as a shift in sense.
